{"type":"message","id":"82d90875","parentId":"a2005e44","timestamp":"2026-05-18T06:07:48.808Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_L6d5pseVVNdmS6cOQZE6j1ey","toolName":"process","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I’m pulling the current RSS feeds and looking for one story that’s clearly breaking across multiple sources today.\nI’ve got a strong candidate already. I’m checking the other feed and then I’ll verify the story is fresh before I extract the full article details.\nI found the main thread: Bitcoin weakness under $79K, with ETF outflows and macro pressure showing up in multiple feeds. I’m grabbing the exact articles and validating the URLs now.\nI’ve got the matching titles; now I’m pulling the article bodies and making sure I don’t accidentally use a stale or duplicate story.\nI have enough to proceed on the Bitcoin drawdown story. I’m extracting the article text now so the JSON is grounded in the actual reporting, not just the feed snippets.\nOne source is rate-limiting the fetch, so I’m switching to the cleanest available article text and using that with the feed data from the other source.\nI’ve got enough raw material from Decrypt. I’m doing one final pass on the CoinDesk/Google angle for corroboration, then I’ll package the single-topic JSON cleanly.\n{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin selloff under $79K as Treasury-yield pressure and ETF outflows weigh on crypto\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin Slides Below $79K as Treasury Yields and ETF Outflows Hit Risk Assets\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-price-dives-under-79k-us-bond-market-triggers-3-btc-price-rout?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/367952/bitcoin-shrugs-off-clarity-gains-as-institutions-sell-amid-surging-treasury-yields\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph reported that Bitcoin fell below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, with traders watching a support retest and downside targets in the mid-$70,000 zone as bond-market stress hit risk assets.\",\n    \"Decrypt quoted HashKey Group senior researcher Tim Sun saying the options market points to resistance between $82,000 and $84,000 and support at $77,000, warning that a break below $77,000 with elevated perpetual swap open interest could trigger a deleveraging phase.\",\n    \"Decrypt also quoted B2PRIME Group CSO Alex Tsepaev saying U.S. Treasury yields above 4.5% and pricing out of Fed cuts are pushing allocations toward cash and bonds, while ETF outflows could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin’s latest weakness is being framed across multiple crypto outlets as a macro-driven risk-off move rather than an isolated crypto problem. Cointelegraph’s coverage said Bitcoin was sliding under the key $79,000 level and, in the companion market analysis, that BTC had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May. The article tied the move to a bond-market shock and higher Treasury yields, saying the market is starting to react after a stretch of euphoria. It quoted the Kobeissi Letter saying the current situation in the bond market is unsustainable, and highlighted that U.S. 10-year Treasury yields had moved above levels seen in April 2025 when President Donald Trump paused China tariff implementation due to what the post called “a collapsing bond market.” The same report said the market was now seeing a 60%+ chance that the Fed’s next move is an interest-rate hike, with rate cuts effectively priced out. The post added that 7%+ mortgages could be next and that auto-loan delinquencies have reached 32-year highs, reinforcing the idea that inflation and higher rates are back in focus. On the Bitcoin side, Cointelegraph said traders were already unsure BTC could push beyond the $82,000 local highs, with support retests back on the radar and downside targets extending toward the mid-$70,000 zone. It also quoted trader Pat saying it was “not a good sign” that BTC fully retraced the previous day’s move, while analyst Eric Coleman said price action had become rangebound between horizontal support and resistance until a clear breakout or breakdown occurs.\\n\\nDecrypt’s story on the same theme sharpened the same pressure points with more explicit levels and market structure commentary. The piece said Bitcoin was shrugging off gains tied to the U.S. CLARITY Act because institutions were selling amid surging Treasury yields. Tim Sun, senior researcher at HashKey Group, told Decrypt that funding rates remained generally moderate and the long/short ratio had not reached extremes, but the options market showed a clear resistance zone between $82,000 and $84,000 with downside support at $77,000. Sun warned that if Bitcoin holds the level, ETF outflows would likely create short-term volatility rather than a full trend reversal; if Bitcoin breaks below $77,000 while perpetual swap open interest remains high, the market could enter a deleveraging phase and deepen the decline. Decrypt also quoted Alex Tsepaev, chief strategy officer at B2PRIME Group, who said that when U.S. Treasury yields are above 4.5% and the market prices out future Fed cuts, some allocations naturally shift toward cash and bonds. Tsepaev’s base case was zero rate cuts this year, though one late cut in November or December could still happen if inflation and labor markets weaken. The article added that Myriad users were assigning only a 4% chance that the Fed would cut rates by more than 25 bps before July. On ETF flows, Tsepaev said ETF selling alone would not erase the broader picture but could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area. Decrypt concluded that Bitcoin’s ability to hold above $77,000 would determine whether the outflows remained a short-term headwind or developed into something more damaging.\\n\\nTaken together, the reporting paints a consistent picture: Bitcoin is under pressure because bond yields, expected Fed policy, and ETF flow dynamics are all leaning against risk assets at the same time. The immediate market focus is on whether BTC can stabilize above the $77,000 support area or whether a further break forces a new round of deleveraging. Across both stories, the $82,000 to $84,000 area is the near-term resistance band, while $76,000 to $77,000 is the zone that keeps showing up as the key downside shelf. The tone in both pieces is cautious but not panicked: analysts are watching for a technical unwind, but they also note that funding rates are not yet extreme and that short-term volatility may still dominate before any larger trend decision is made.\"\n}\nCHART_COIN: bitcoin\n\nProcess exited with code 0."}],"details":{"status":"completed","sessionId":"delta-sable","exitCode":0,"aggregated":"I’m pulling the current RSS feeds and looking for one story that’s clearly breaking across multiple sources today.\nI’ve got a strong candidate already. I’m checking the other feed and then I’ll verify the story is fresh before I extract the full article details.\nI found the main thread: Bitcoin weakness under $79K, with ETF outflows and macro pressure showing up in multiple feeds. I’m grabbing the exact articles and validating the URLs now.\nI’ve got the matching titles; now I’m pulling the article bodies and making sure I don’t accidentally use a stale or duplicate story.\nI have enough to proceed on the Bitcoin drawdown story. I’m extracting the article text now so the JSON is grounded in the actual reporting, not just the feed snippets.\nOne source is rate-limiting the fetch, so I’m switching to the cleanest available article text and using that with the feed data from the other source.\nI’ve got enough raw material from Decrypt. I’m doing one final pass on the CoinDesk/Google angle for corroboration, then I’ll package the single-topic JSON cleanly.\n{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin selloff under $79K as Treasury-yield pressure and ETF outflows weigh on crypto\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin Slides Below $79K as Treasury Yields and ETF Outflows Hit Risk Assets\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-price-dives-under-79k-us-bond-market-triggers-3-btc-price-rout?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/367952/bitcoin-shrugs-off-clarity-gains-as-institutions-sell-amid-surging-treasury-yields\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph reported that Bitcoin fell below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, with traders watching a support retest and downside targets in the mid-$70,000 zone as bond-market stress hit risk assets.\",\n    \"Decrypt quoted HashKey Group senior researcher Tim Sun saying the options market points to resistance between $82,000 and $84,000 and support at $77,000, warning that a break below $77,000 with elevated perpetual swap open interest could trigger a deleveraging phase.\",\n    \"Decrypt also quoted B2PRIME Group CSO Alex Tsepaev saying U.S. Treasury yields above 4.5% and pricing out of Fed cuts are pushing allocations toward cash and bonds, while ETF outflows could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin’s latest weakness is being framed across multiple crypto outlets as a macro-driven risk-off move rather than an isolated crypto problem. Cointelegraph’s coverage said Bitcoin was sliding under the key $79,000 level and, in the companion market analysis, that BTC had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May. The article tied the move to a bond-market shock and higher Treasury yields, saying the market is starting to react after a stretch of euphoria. It quoted the Kobeissi Letter saying the current situation in the bond market is unsustainable, and highlighted that U.S. 10-year Treasury yields had moved above levels seen in April 2025 when President Donald Trump paused China tariff implementation due to what the post called “a collapsing bond market.” The same report said the market was now seeing a 60%+ chance that the Fed’s next move is an interest-rate hike, with rate cuts effectively priced out. The post added that 7%+ mortgages could be next and that auto-loan delinquencies have reached 32-year highs, reinforcing the idea that inflation and higher rates are back in focus. On the Bitcoin side, Cointelegraph said traders were already unsure BTC could push beyond the $82,000 local highs, with support retests back on the radar and downside targets extending toward the mid-$70,000 zone. It also quoted trader Pat saying it was “not a good sign” that BTC fully retraced the previous day’s move, while analyst Eric Coleman said price action had become rangebound between horizontal support and resistance until a clear breakout or breakdown occurs.\\n\\nDecrypt’s story on the same theme sharpened the same pressure points with more explicit levels and market structure commentary. The piece said Bitcoin was shrugging off gains tied to the U.S. CLARITY Act because institutions were selling amid surging Treasury yields. Tim Sun, senior researcher at HashKey Group, told Decrypt that funding rates remained generally moderate and the long/short ratio had not reached extremes, but the options market showed a clear resistance zone between $82,000 and $84,000 with downside support at $77,000. Sun warned that if Bitcoin holds the level, ETF outflows would likely create short-term volatility rather than a full trend reversal; if Bitcoin breaks below $77,000 while perpetual swap open interest remains high, the market could enter a deleveraging phase and deepen the decline. Decrypt also quoted Alex Tsepaev, chief strategy officer at B2PRIME Group, who said that when U.S. Treasury yields are above 4.5% and the market prices out future Fed cuts, some allocations naturally shift toward cash and bonds. Tsepaev’s base case was zero rate cuts this year, though one late cut in November or December could still happen if inflation and labor markets weaken. The article added that Myriad users were assigning only a 4% chance that the Fed would cut rates by more than 25 bps before July. On ETF flows, Tsepaev said ETF selling alone would not erase the broader picture but could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area. Decrypt concluded that Bitcoin’s ability to hold above $77,000 would determine whether the outflows remained a short-term headwind or developed into something more damaging.\\n\\nTaken together, the reporting paints a consistent picture: Bitcoin is under pressure because bond yields, expected Fed policy, and ETF flow dynamics are all leaning against risk assets at the same time. The immediate market focus is on whether BTC can stabilize above the $77,000 support area or whether a further break forces a new round of deleveraging. Across both stories, the $82,000 to $84,000 area is the near-term resistance band, while $76,000 to $77,000 is the zone that keeps showing up as the key downside shelf. The tone in both pieces is cautious but not panicked: analysts are watching for a technical unwind, but they also note that funding rates are not yet extreme and that short-term volatility may still dominate before any larger trend decision is made.\"\n}\nCHART_COIN: bitcoin\n","name":"rm ~/.openclaw/agents/res...sessions/sessions.json"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779084468808}}
{"type":"message","id":"b2cc9451","parentId":"82d90875","timestamp":"2026-05-18T06:08:18.575Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"✅ Research done, starting article..."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_fP8TI5TQ2s2xcKOwi5YBEEGW","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\nopenclaw agent --agent writer --message \"First, read the COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md file in your workspace to learn your strict formatting rules. Then, write a 1100-1200 word crypto news article using these facts: {\\\"topic_theme\\\":\\\"Bitcoin selloff under $79K as Treasury-yield pressure and ETF outflows weigh on crypto\\\",\\\"primary_keyword\\\":\\\"Bitcoin selloff\\\",\\\"primary_headline\\\":\\\"Bitcoin Slides Below $79K as Treasury Yields and ETF Outflows Hit Risk Assets\\\",\\\"sources_used\\\":[\\\"Cointelegraph\\\",\\\"Decrypt\\\"],\\\"source_urls\\\":[\\\"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-price-dives-under-79k-us-bond-market-triggers-3-btc-price-rout?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\\",\\\"https://decrypt.co/367952/bitcoin-shrugs-off-clarity-gains-as-institutions-sell-amid-surging-treasury-yields\\\"],\\\"combined_key_facts\\\":[\\\"Cointelegraph reported that Bitcoin fell below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, with traders watching a support retest and downside targets in the mid-$70,000 zone as bond-market stress hit risk assets.\\\",\\\"Decrypt quoted HashKey Group senior researcher Tim Sun saying the options market points to resistance between $82,000 and $84,000 and support at $77,000, warning that a break below $77,000 with elevated perpetual swap open interest could trigger a deleveraging phase.\\\",\\\"Decrypt also quoted B2PRIME Group CSO Alex Tsepaev saying U.S. Treasury yields above 4.5% and pricing out of Fed cuts are pushing allocations toward cash and bonds, while ETF outflows could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area.\\\"],\\\"aggregated_raw_content\\\":\\\"Bitcoin’s latest weakness is being framed across multiple crypto outlets as a macro-driven risk-off move rather than an isolated crypto problem. Cointelegraph’s coverage said Bitcoin was sliding under the key $79,000 level and, in the companion market analysis, that BTC had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May. The article tied the move to a bond-market shock and higher Treasury yields, saying the market is starting to react after a stretch of euphoria. It quoted the Kobeissi Letter saying the current situation in the bond market is unsustainable, and highlighted that U.S. 10-year Treasury yields had moved above levels seen in April 2025 when President Donald Trump paused China tariff implementation due to what the post called ‘a collapsing bond market.’ The same report said the market was now seeing a 60%+ chance that the Fed’s next move is an interest-rate hike, with rate cuts effectively priced out. The post added that 7%+ mortgages could be next and that auto-loan delinquencies have reached 32-year highs, reinforcing the idea that inflation and higher rates are back in focus. On the Bitcoin side, Cointelegraph said traders were already unsure BTC could push beyond the $82,000 local highs, with support retests back on the radar and downside targets extending toward the mid-$70,000 zone. It also quoted trader Pat saying it was ‘not a good sign’ that BTC fully retraced the previous day’s move, while analyst Eric Coleman said price action had become rangebound between horizontal support and resistance until a clear breakout or breakdown occurs.\\\\n\\\\nDecrypt’s story on the same theme sharpened the same pressure points with more explicit levels and market structure commentary. The piece said Bitcoin was shrugging off gains tied to the U.S. CLARITY Act because institutions were selling amid surging Treasury yields. Tim Sun, senior researcher at HashKey Group, told Decrypt that funding rates remained generally moderate and the long/short ratio had not reached extremes, but the options market showed a clear resistance zone between $82,000 and $84,000 with downside support at $77,000. Sun warned that if Bitcoin holds the level, ETF outflows would likely create short-term volatility rather than a full trend reversal; if Bitcoin breaks below $77,000 while perpetual swap open interest remains high, the market could enter a deleveraging phase and deepen the decline. Decrypt also quoted Alex Tsepaev, chief strategy officer at B2PRIME Group, who said that when U.S. Treasury yields are above 4.5% and the market prices out future Fed cuts, some allocations naturally shift toward cash and bonds. Tsepaev’s base case was zero rate cuts this year, though one late cut in November or December could still happen if inflation and labor markets weaken. The article added that Myriad users were assigning only a 4% chance that the Fed would cut rates by more than 25 bps before July. On ETF flows, Tsepaev said ETF selling alone would not erase the broader picture but could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area. Decrypt concluded that Bitcoin’s ability to hold above $77,000 would determine whether the outflows remained a short-term headwind or developed into something more damaging.\\\\n\\\\nTaken together, the reporting paints a consistent picture: Bitcoin is under pressure because bond yields, expected Fed policy, and ETF flow dynamics are all leaning against risk assets at the same time. The immediate market focus is on whether BTC can stabilize above the $77,000 support area or whether a further break forces a new round of deleveraging. Across both stories, the $82,000 to $84,000 area is the near-term resistance band, while $76,000 to $77,000 is the zone that keeps showing up as the key downside shelf. The tone in both pieces is cautious but not panicked: analysts are watching for a technical unwind, but they also note that funding rates are not yet extreme and that short-term volatility may still dominate before any larger trend decision is made.\\\"}. You MUST finish the article completely, ending with the Sources and Word Count block.\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\nsed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\n\nif ! grep -qi \"Word Count:\" /tmp/crypto-article.md; then\n  echo \"Article incomplete. Forcing recovery run...\"\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \"Your previous attempt was cut off. Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md and write a tighter article using these facts: {\\\"topic_theme\\\":\\\"Bitcoin selloff under $79K as Treasury-yield pressure and ETF outflows weigh on crypto\\\",\\\"primary_keyword\\\":\\\"Bitcoin selloff\\\",\\\"primary_headline\\\":\\\"Bitcoin Slides Below $79K as Treasury Yields and ETF Outflows Hit Risk Assets\\\",\\\"sources_used\\\":[\\\"Cointelegraph\\\",\\\"Decrypt\\\"],\\\"source_urls\\\":[\\\"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-price-dives-under-79k-us-bond-market-triggers-3-btc-price-rout?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\\",\\\"https://decrypt.co/367952/bitcoin-shrugs-off-clarity-gains-as-institutions-sell-amid-surging-treasury-yields\\\"],\\\"combined_key_facts\\\":[\\\"Cointelegraph reported that Bitcoin fell below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, with traders watching a support retest and downside targets in the mid-$70,000 zone as bond-market stress hit risk assets.\\\",\\\"Decrypt quoted HashKey Group senior researcher Tim Sun saying the options market points to resistance between $82,000 and $84,000 and support at $77,000, warning that a break below $77,000 with elevated perpetual swap open interest could trigger a deleveraging phase.\\\",\\\"Decrypt also quoted B2PRIME Group CSO Alex Tsepaev saying U.S. Treasury yields above 4.5% and pricing out of Fed cuts are pushing allocations toward cash and bonds, while ETF outflows could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area.\\\"],\\\"aggregated_raw_content\\\":\\\"Bitcoin’s latest weakness is being framed across multiple crypto outlets as a macro-driven risk-off move rather than an isolated crypto problem. Cointelegraph’s coverage said Bitcoin was sliding under the key $79,000 level and, in the companion market analysis, that BTC had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May. The article tied the move to a bond-market shock and higher Treasury yields, saying the market is starting to react after a stretch of euphoria. It quoted the Kobeissi Letter saying the current situation in the bond market is unsustainable, and highlighted that U.S. 10-year Treasury yields had moved above levels seen in April 2025 when President Donald Trump paused China tariff implementation due to what the post called ‘a collapsing bond market.’ The same report said the market was now seeing a 60%+ chance that the Fed’s next move is an interest-rate hike, with rate cuts effectively priced out. The post added that 7%+ mortgages could be next and that auto-loan delinquencies have reached 32-year highs, reinforcing the idea that inflation and higher rates are back in focus. On the Bitcoin side, Cointelegraph said traders were already unsure BTC could push beyond the $82,000 local highs, with support retests back on the radar and downside targets extending toward the mid-$70,000 zone. It also quoted trader Pat saying it was ‘not a good sign’ that BTC fully retraced the previous day’s move, while analyst Eric Coleman said price action had become rangebound between horizontal support and resistance until a clear breakout or breakdown occurs.\\\\n\\\\nDecrypt’s story on the same theme sharpened the same pressure points with more explicit levels and market structure commentary. The piece said Bitcoin was shrugging off gains tied to the U.S. CLARITY Act because institutions were selling amid surging Treasury yields. Tim Sun, senior researcher at HashKey Group, told Decrypt that funding rates remained generally moderate and the long/short ratio had not reached extremes, but the options market showed a clear resistance zone between $82,000 and $84,000 with downside support at $77,000. Sun warned that if Bitcoin holds the level, ETF outflows would likely create short-term volatility rather than a full trend reversal; if Bitcoin breaks below $77,000 while perpetual swap open interest remains high, the market could enter a deleveraging phase and deepen the decline. Decrypt also quoted Alex Tsepaev, chief strategy officer at B2PRIME Group, who said that when U.S. Treasury yields are above 4.5% and the market prices out future Fed cuts, some allocations naturally shift toward cash and bonds. Tsepaev’s base case was zero rate cuts this year, though one late cut in November or December could still happen if inflation and labor markets weaken. The article added that Myriad users were assigning only a 4% chance that the Fed would cut rates by more than 25 bps before July. On ETF flows, Tsepaev said ETF selling alone would not erase the broader picture but could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area. Decrypt concluded that Bitcoin’s ability to hold above $77,000 would determine whether the outflows remained a short-term headwind or developed into something more damaging.\\\\n\\\\nTaken together, the reporting paints a consistent picture: Bitcoin is under pressure because bond yields, expected Fed policy, and ETF flow dynamics are all leaning against risk assets at the same time. The immediate market focus is on whether BTC can stabilize above the $77,000 support area or whether a further break forces a new round of deleveraging. Across both stories, the $82,000 to $84,000 area is the near-term resistance band, while $76,000 to $77,000 is the zone that keeps showing up as the key downside shelf. The tone in both pieces is cautious but not panicked: analysts are watching for a technical unwind, but they also note that funding rates are not yet extreme and that short-term volatility may still dominate before any larger trend decision is made.\\\"}. You MUST reach the final Word Count block.\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\nfi\n\nword_count=$(wc -w < /tmp/crypto-article.md)\nif [ \"$word_count\" -gt 1500 ]; then\n  echo \"Article is too long ($word_count words). Forcing summarization pass...\"\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \"Your previous draft was mathematically too long ($word_count words). Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md. Condense and summarize this exact text to be roughly 1100 words. Keep the same structure but shorten every paragraph aggressively.\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\n  mv /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md /tmp/crypto-article.md\nfi","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator","yieldMs":1000,"timeout":900,"host":"auto","security":"full","ask":"on-miss"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1565,"output":2965,"cacheRead":14080,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":18610,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779084468809,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DglQmAkrJjlASz7g7Rd2BtHIimkt5"}}
